- Constructor chaining - whenever an instance of child class is created, during that time, constructor of first parent in hierarchy is called
following till that child class. There is super() keyword already associated (by default if not present even) calling the parent class
constructor.

- this keyword refers to the current instance or current object address. So if there are two instances at present with different value
therefore.

- Which statement correctly uses the this keyword to call another constructor in the same class?
this() - is used to call another constructor within the same class. It must be the first statement inside the constructor.

class Example {
    Example() {
        this(10); // calling another constructor
    }

    Example(int x) {
        System.out.println(x);
    }
}

